MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The 'gg' class was used to avoid a weird problem with overloaded
operators and S3 methods. If you define methods `+.a` and `+.b`, and
then try to add an object of class a with an object of class b, R
doesn't like it -- even when the two methods are identical.

This came about because of the new theming system. Previously, you
couldn't add themes to each other; you had to add them to a ggplot
object. The new system allows adding themes objects to other theme
objects, without a ggplot object getting involved, and that's where
the operator issues occurred. The gg class was a workaround for the
problem. Now the `+.gg` operator looks at the class of its inputs and
sends them off to the proper function.

If you're curious to know more about the problem:
http://r.789695.n4.nabble.com/quot-Incompatible-methods-quot-for-overloaded-operator-td4633362.html

I think this should be stable in the future.

-Winston

