>>>>
>>>> Criteria:
>>>>
>>>> * algorithmic (can be generated from code, specifically R)
>>>> * leverage existing ggplot look/identity
>>>> * focused on on-line presentation
>>>>
>>>> How the criteria influenced the design:
>>>>
>>>> ggplot2 has a distinctive look to how it produces data graphics. However,
>>>> that is not so helpful for a logo. Further, what makes a good data graphic
>>>> and what makes a good logo or icon is not the same. So I didn't try to make
>>>> the logo using ggplot itself. However, grid does offer enough control that
>>>> it does make a reasonable medium. Also, since ggplot uses grid, there is a
>>>> familial relationship.
>>>>
>>>> The useful iconographic graphical aspects of ggplot which are useful are
>>>> the icons on the (old) documentation site for the different geoms and
>>>> stats. These have the additional advantage of being rendered using grid and
>>>> so that can be leveraged.
>>>>
>>>> For online applications, a square icon works best. That also makes using
>>>> the older documentation icons easier since they are already square.
>>>>
>>>> Design:
>>>>
>>>> I took the icon for facet_grid (what I though as a most distinctive
>>>> aspect of ggplot) as the base, and then inside each of the 4 squares, used
>>>> different icons: scale_grey, geom_point, geom_dotplot, and scale_size.
